#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325668 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0403251a-9491-5d04-ab5a-ed027024d0a4" :knowledge-point/title "Inverting Affine Transformations Given in Matrix Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e2490baf-fe52-5d66-b93e-5369d7b86971" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325670} {:answer/id #uuid "6eab3492-9d9d-5111-9619-ba096f1e7b6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325671} {:answer/id #uuid "b2ca837f-ffa8-5ad5-9d65-7313bb5bae79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325672} {:answer/id #uuid "037794be-432e-5662-97ac-f60f53a945b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325673} {:answer/id #uuid "53d010b3-2900-51ea-a5c2-021a4e0ec9de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325674}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6eab3492-9d9d-5111-9619-ba096f1e7b6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325671} :answer-field/id #uuid "50b86d78-627a-5db8-82a1-925eba6995b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325669}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5d8f9018-e49a-5dc6-b30f-3f20cff0c3d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-124224" :question/problem "The matrix form of the affine transformation $T$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind $a+b+c,$ given that the matrix form of the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ a & b\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ c\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix form of $T$ gives us the following information:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{-1\\cdot (-9)-2\\cdot 4}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -9\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -20\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=-4+(-9)+20=7.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325675 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0403251a-9491-5d04-ab5a-ed027024d0a4" :knowledge-point/title "Inverting Affine Transformations Given in Matrix Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b835c673-3eed-5efd-838b-a60cb3b41e8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325677} {:answer/id #uuid "28cabba1-5cfb-5518-8cb0-5a10bfd6138a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325678} {:answer/id #uuid "1d6efe97-0354-5d38-86f2-3083fb1cf506" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325679} {:answer/id #uuid "047d7661-add7-57f7-8f41-87c644bd2ecf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325680} {:answer/id #uuid "920e8c27-583f-51c9-9add-af7cd67a13d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325681}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "047d7661-add7-57f7-8f41-87c644bd2ecf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325680} :answer-field/id #uuid "2bb3df9f-9bef-5c0a-a068-b10974aff81e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325676}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a8bbef2f-90e2-5e6c-8433-de5f1145da6f" :question/math-academy-id "q-124238" :question/problem "The matrix form of the affine transformation $T$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind $a+b+c,$ given that the matrix form of the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}a & -3 \\\\ 1 & b\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ c\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix form of $T$ gives us the following information:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{6\\cdot (-5)-(-9)\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}6 & 9 \\\\ -3 & -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-3}[\\begin{aligned}6 & 9 \\\\ -3 & -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & \\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & \\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & \\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & \\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & \\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & \\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=-2+\\frac{5}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325682 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b99071b9-d463-5e3e-9a0d-71711af836cb" :knowledge-point/title "Inverting Affine Transformations Given in Parametric Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c93ca25a-7096-5f43-ac3d-973bfc1ec997" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325684} {:answer/id #uuid "9a10a3bf-766e-59fe-94bb-cfa1e46c680d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325685} {:answer/id #uuid "1de18a43-6575-5d60-8781-0dc5a9e52d9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325686} {:answer/id #uuid "60c0e35f-9689-5c9d-aa56-c17bf34f1bd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325687} {:answer/id #uuid "e41b07b3-1d88-544f-a9f9-6c2505be6e17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325688}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e41b07b3-1d88-544f-a9f9-6c2505be6e17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325688} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6643577-fa44-52f8-9cdb-dcd7c980bb11" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325683}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2bbe5c8f-5f13-5d77-bcba-0616b7c68af8" :question/math-academy-id "q-124282" :question/problem "The affine transformation $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nx=2u-v+1,\\,y=9u-4v+3.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ of $T$ is\n\n$$\nu=y-4x+a,\\,v=by+cx+3,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing our transformation in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2u-v+1 \\\\ 9u-4v+3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2u-v \\\\ 9u-4v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 9 & -4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{-4\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot 9}[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 1 \\\\ -9 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 1 \\\\ -9 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 1 \\\\ -9 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 1 \\\\ -9 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y-3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4\\cdot (x-1)+1\\cdot (y-3) \\\\ -9\\cdot (x-1)+2\\cdot (y-3)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}y-4x+1 \\\\ 2y-9x+3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ in coordinate form is\n\n$$\nu=y-4x+1,\\,v=2y+(-9)x+3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=1+2+(-9)=-6.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325689 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b99071b9-d463-5e3e-9a0d-71711af836cb" :knowledge-point/title "Inverting Affine Transformations Given in Parametric Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d92c27be-527c-57fd-9e7e-653f5f39e7b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325691} {:answer/id #uuid "76c7b074-cbb9-519f-bd54-b758d8a9b28d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325692} {:answer/id #uuid "af0e3026-8057-52ee-88cb-8ffa5e15f5c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325693} {:answer/id #uuid "219a0561-e4ee-5862-b114-650255f518a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325694} {:answer/id #uuid "c4f3a176-e1bf-58a5-8fad-eb248e09eb4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325695}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c4f3a176-e1bf-58a5-8fad-eb248e09eb4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325695} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0594b9f-57bb-5cf4-aa0c-07bd9fdbd552" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325690}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "be9e4836-1dc9-5427-b7c3-1f0fa45b5e48" :question/math-academy-id "q-124316" :question/problem "The affine transformation $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nx=-2v-4,\\,y=u+4v+6.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ of $T$ is\n\n$$\nu=2x+y+a,\\,v=bx+cy-2,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing our transformation in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2v-4 \\\\ u+4v+6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2v \\\\ u+4v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{4\\cdot 0-(-2)\\cdot 1}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x+4 \\\\ y-6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot (x+4)+1\\cdot (y-6) \\\\ (-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot (x+4)+0\\cdot (y-6)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2x+y+2 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}x-2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ in coordinate form is\n\n$$\nu=2x+y+2,\\,v=(-\\frac{1}{2})x+0y-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=2+(-\\frac{1}{2})+0=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325696 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e551b65-4116-58bc-b2bc-e536930c69b4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Types of Images Formed by an Affine Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c8bf0bf9-8a03-5d14-965f-090519b9e41a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A parallelogram" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325698} {:answer/id #uuid "13a809a1-3dd8-5138-821d-f17c0640dd3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A square" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325699} {:answer/id #uuid "02dea30c-e499-5b84-9b33-e9bc84a9052a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325700} {:answer/id #uuid "f7b78b86-45af-57c1-a3f5-d5ad334a5415" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A triangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325701} {:answer/id #uuid "a8dbf22b-d9d3-5d1e-be19-4070c40a3e22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A line segment" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325702}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7b78b86-45af-57c1-a3f5-d5ad334a5415" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A triangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325701} :answer-field/id #uuid "e33aa621-7bf7-5e3c-85b4-e4e4c863cdb3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325697}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3018f96f-b9d5-5624-96d5-6e8a702124a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-162217" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045422/assets/c1ee8bc2c212708ed583c709420a856b28f6e2129c24690a504b4716b2692aa7.png)\n\nConsider the affine transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nx=u-2v+1,\\,y=2u-3v+1.\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any affine transformation \n\n$$\nT:u⟶Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $u$ and $b$ are $2\\times 1$ column vectors, we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., when $det(A)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., when $det(A)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $A$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $A$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a point on the plane.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nThe given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}u-2v+1 \\\\ 2u-3v+1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}u-2v \\\\ 2u-3v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}].$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-3)-2\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =1 \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a non-singular matrix. \n\nTherefore, $T$ is invertible and the image of the triangle under the action of $T$ is a triangle."}]}
